1
00:00:01,130 --> 00:00:04,820
Here's a sample timeline we
built of the United States,

2
00:00:04,870 --> 00:00:08,790
from the first shots of
the Revolution to 2008.

3
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:12,740
Wars and movements are bands,
everything else is a moment,

4
00:00:12,790 --> 00:00:16,310
and each one links to
the article it draws on.

5
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:18,897
Chapter headings mark the eras.

6
00:00:20,447 --> 00:00:25,197
Zoom into the 1860s and
the Civil War opens up.

7
00:00:25,247 --> 00:00:30,077
Fort Sumter, the Emancipation Proclamation,
the three days of Gettysburg,

8
00:00:30,127 --> 00:00:34,077
Lincoln's address there that November,
the surrender at Appomattox,

9
00:00:34,127 --> 00:00:39,937
and five days later, his assassination.
At this distance you can see how close

10
00:00:39,987 --> 00:00:41,619
together they were.

11
00:00:43,329 --> 00:00:47,429
Pull one thread out of the
rest. Separate by tags,

12
00:00:47,479 --> 00:00:52,549
choose rights, and every moment about who
the founding promises applied to moves

13
00:00:52,599 --> 00:00:55,159
into a track of its own:
from the Declaration,

14
00:00:55,209 --> 00:00:59,809
through the amendments after the Civil
War, to Brown versus Board and the Civil

15
00:00:59,859 --> 00:01:01,188
Rights Act.

16
00:01:02,738 --> 00:01:07,738
Open a moment to read it. Brown
versus Board of Education:

17
00:01:07,788 --> 00:01:12,368
what the Court decided, where that
comes from, and what it's linked to.

18
00:01:12,418 --> 00:01:15,278
Follow a link to the Civil
Rights Act ten years later,

19
00:01:15,328 --> 00:01:16,830
and keep going.

20
00:01:18,380 --> 00:01:23,770
And the same line goes all the way down.
Zoom from two and a half centuries to a

21
00:01:23,820 --> 00:01:30,160
single night in July 1969: the Eagle
lands at seventeen minutes and forty

22
00:01:30,210 --> 00:01:34,200
seconds past eight, UTC, and
six and a half hours later,

23
00:01:34,250 --> 00:01:35,478
the first step.

24
00:01:37,028 --> 00:01:41,898
Anyone can read a public timeline
like this, no account needed.

25
00:01:41,948 --> 00:01:45,318
Build one with your class, or have
each student build their own,

26
00:01:45,368 --> 00:01:48,374
and share it with a link
or on your class page.
